    "group": "201-300",
    "title": "Lowest Common Ancestor of a BST",
    "workload": "build one 200000-node BST from LCG values (randomly shaped, depth ~35; a sorted input would degenerate it into a 200000-deep chain), then 8000000 root-to-split LCA walks. Query pairs are drawn from the inserted values so both endpoints are always present. The query count is high because a single walk is only O(h) and the tree build is a fixed setup cost that would otherwise dominate. Sink accumulates the LCA values mod 1e9+7. NOTE: all four lanes use an index-pool tree (array of structs with i64 child indices, -1 = null) rather than pointer-linked nodes, so pointer-chasing behaviour matches; the bench builds iteratively where the kata inserts recursively, which is a setup-only difference.",
